            <div xml:id="d779">

                <pb facs="0723" n="711" sameAs="frus:frus1944v05/pg711"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">890F.24/6–2944: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister Resident in Saudi Arabia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Moose</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Jidda</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-06-29T09:00:00+03:06">June 29, 1944—9
                            a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 1:30 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>185. Yesterday morning British Chargé d’Affaires and I together called on Acting
                    Foreign Minister. I repeated verbally substance of Department’s 1536 to Cairo,
                    June 20, 6 p.m., and of similar message from Landis (Cairo’s June 22, 8 a.m.)
                    which had already been communicated in writing. British Chargé stated that his
                    Government in replying to King’s message had expressed hope of prompt solution
                    of Saudi supply problem and had added that necessary instructions were already
                    on their way to Cairo.</p>
                <p>Importance of this communication can only be judged on disclosure of nature of
                    instructions about which British Chargé knows nothing.</p>
                <p>Sent Department. Repeated to Cairo for Landis.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moose</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
